CHAPTERS = [
dict(title="A Quiet Night in Edo", scene="nihonbashi", paras=[
"Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive. Tonight we travel to Edo, the great city that would one day be called Tokyo, on a cool autumn evening about two hundred years ago. Nothing dramatic will happen. We will only listen to the temple bells counting the hours, and watch a vast wooden city put out its lamps, one by one.",
"Settle yourself the way you like to sleep. Let your shoulders sink and your hands go loose. Take one slow breath in, through the nose, and imagine the air is cool and faintly sweet, with a little woodsmoke in it, and the smell of the river. Then let the breath go, slowly, all the way out.",
"You can follow along, or you can simply let the words drift past you like boats on dark water. There is nothing to learn and nothing to remember. If you fall asleep before the last bell, the night will carry on quietly without you, exactly as it should.",
]),

dict(title="The Sunset Bell", scene="nihonbashi", paras=[
"We begin on a riverbank in the very heart of the city, beside a long wooden bridge that rises in a gentle arch over the water. This is Nihonbashi, the Bridge of Japan. The first one was built here in 1603, the year the city became the seat of the shogun, and it has been rebuilt more than once since then, always in wood, always with the same soft hump in the middle.",
"Every great road in the country begins on this bridge. Five highways run out from here, along the coast, over the mountains, and up into the north, and every distance in Edo is counted from the planks beneath your feet. A traveller setting off for distant Kyoto would start walking right here, with a straw hat and a small bundle, and a journey of many days ahead.",
"From the high middle of the bridge, if you turn to the west, you can see the dark roofs of the shogun's castle rising above its walls and trees. And far beyond it, floating over the hills, there is the pale cone of Mount Fuji, still holding a little rose-coloured light on its snowy top, long after the streets below have fallen into shadow.",
"Now a sound comes across the rooftops. Three slow strokes on a great bronze bell, spaced well apart, to tell the listeners that the hour is coming. Then the hour itself, rung out stroke after stroke, deep and round. This is the bell at Hongokucho, close by, the oldest of the time bells in Edo. It is announcing sunset.",
"In Edo, the hours are not the same length all year round. The day is split into six parts from dawn to dusk, and the night into six parts from dusk to dawn. So in summer the daylight hours are long and the night hours short, and now, in autumn, the night hours are slowly stretching out, a little longer every evening.",
"The ringing does not stop with this one bell. Far off, another temple takes up the hour, and then another beyond that, each placed just within hearing of the last. Nine of them, in a great ring around the castle. You can hear the sound travel away from you across the city, getting softer and softer, until you are not sure whether you hear it at all.",
"Beside the bridge, the fish market that was so loud this morning is quiet now. The wooden stalls are rinsed and empty, the baskets stacked and drying, the buckets turned upside down. A few gulls stand on the rail of a moored boat, looking out at the water. One of them tucks its head away and goes to sleep.",
"On the far bank, a long row of white-walled storehouses stands along the river, their thick plaster walls glowing faintly in the dusk, their heavy doors shut for the night. And on the bridge itself, people are hurrying home now, wooden sandals clicking on the boards, and here and there someone unfolds a paper lantern and lights it with care.",
"Along the riverbank, the cargo boats are tied up for the night, low and broad, loaded with straw bales of rice, barrels of soy sauce, and bundles of firewood brought in from the countryside. Their boatmen sit on the decks with their long thin pipes, a thread of tobacco smoke rising from each one. One of them taps his pipe out against the side of the boat, three soft knocks, and tucks it into his sash.",
"Above the city, the sky turns from rose to violet to a deep, clear blue, and the first stars come out over the castle. A flight of wild geese passes high overhead, heading south in a long, loose line, calling softly to one another. In Edo, the coming of the geese is one of the signs of autumn, and a few people pause on the bridge to look up and watch them go.",
]),

dict(title="Lanterns on the River", scene="sumida", paras=[
"Let us follow the water downstream, to where the narrow canal opens into the great Sumida river. Here the river is wide and slow, and the moon has come up over the eastern bank, laying a trembling path of silver across the surface. The air is cooler on the water. You might pull your collar a little closer.",
"A pleasure boat is coming down the river. It has a low wooden roof and walls of paper screens, and the lamps inside make the whole cabin glow like a lantern floating on the water. At the stern, a boatman leans on a long pole, pushes, lifts it, and leans again, in an easy rhythm he has repeated ten thousand times.",
"Inside, a few friends are sitting on cushions around a low table, with small cups of warm rice wine and a dish of grilled fish. They are not talking much. One of them has slid back a screen to look at the moon, and the others have turned to look with him. On autumn nights like this one, people in Edo hire boats for exactly this: to drift, and to look at the moon.",
"Further down, a long wooden bridge crosses the river on dozens of thin legs, so long that its far end disappears into the haze. A few lanterns move slowly along it, like the slow sparks of people walking home. In summer this stretch of river would be crowded with boats, but tonight there are only a handful, spread out and drifting.",
"The story goes that, more than a thousand years ago, two brothers were fishing in this river when they drew up something heavy in their net. It was not a fish, but a small golden statue of the gentle Kannon, the bodhisattva of compassion. They took it home, and a temple grew up around it, upstream at Asakusa. We will visit that temple later tonight.",
"On the near bank, a willow leans out over the water, its long thin branches hanging almost to the surface. A breeze moves through it, and the strands sway together, slowly, one way and then back, like a curtain breathing. Under the willow, a small wooden stall has set up for the night, with a lantern hanging from its roof.",
"From the corner of the stall hangs a little bell of iron, a wind chime with a strip of paper beneath it. When the breeze comes, the paper turns and the chime gives one small clear note, and then another. It is a sign that noodles are being served here, and anyone on the riverbank who hears that sound knows that hot soup is close by.",
"The pleasure boat has drifted on downstream now, its glow growing smaller, its reflection stretching long and gold across the water behind it. The boatman's pole knocks once against the side of the hull. Then there is only the quiet lap of the river against the stone of the bank, and the willow, and the small chime, turning in the wind.",
"The noodle seller is a young man with a cotton cloth tied around his head. He carries his whole kitchen on a pole across his shoulders: at one end a little charcoal stove with a pot of broth, at the other a stack of drawers holding noodles, bowls and chopsticks. A bowl costs sixteen small coins. The noodles are buckwheat, thin and grey-brown, served in a dark, savoury broth with a scattering of chopped spring onion.",
"A ferryman ties up his little boat at the bank and comes over for a bowl. He sits on an upturned bucket, holds the bowl close to his face, and eats slowly, the steam drifting around his cheeks in the cool air. Edo is full of working men who live alone, far from their home villages, and small stalls like this one feed many of them, late into the night.",
]),

dict(title="The Lanes Grow Quiet", scene="lanes", paras=[
"We turn away from the river now, and walk inland, into the merchant quarter. The streets here are lined with wooden shophouses, two storeys high, with dark tiled roofs and deep eaves. The fronts of the shops are made of wooden lattice, and through the slats you can see the warm glow of lamps inside, where the families who keep the shops are finishing their day.",
"Each shop has a curtain hanging over its door, a short length of dyed cloth split into panels, so you can push through it with your hand. It carries the name of the shop, or its sign, in white on deep indigo. All day the curtains hang out over the street. Now, one by one, the shopkeepers are reaching up and taking them in. When the curtain comes down, the shop is closed for the night.",
"Under the eaves of a lantern maker's shop, a dozen round paper lanterns are hanging on hooks, lit to show what he sells. Each one is made of thin split bamboo wound round and round in a spiral, with paper pasted over it, so that when it is not needed, it folds down flat into its own base like a closed fan. They sway a little in the breeze, all slightly out of time with one another.",
"Inside the shop, the lantern maker is cleaning his brushes. He has spent the afternoon painting a family crest onto a new lantern, a circle with three leaves inside, in black ink on the cream paper. It is drying now on a rack by the wall. Tomorrow someone will come to collect it, and carry it home through these same streets.",
"Next door, a seller of tea is folding the cloths that covered his jars. Across the lane, a woman in a plain cotton robe is sweeping the earth in front of her doorway with a short broom, in neat, even strokes, and then she sprinkles a little water from a ladle to settle the dust. It is something people do here every evening, a small courtesy to the street.",
"Halfway down the lane, a tall ladder rises above the rooftops, with a little railed platform at the top and a small bronze bell hanging from a post. It is a fire watchtower. Every few blocks in Edo there is one like it, so that someone can climb up and look out across the roofs. Tonight the air is calm and still, and the bell hangs quiet.",
"At the end of the lane stands a wooden gate, with two heavy doors and a little wicket door set into one of them. Edo is divided into thousands of these small neighbourhoods, each with its own gate, and late in the evening the gates are closed. After that, a late traveller must knock at the wicket, and the old gatekeeper in his little hut will look out, and nod, and let them through.",
"For now, the gate is still open, and people pass through it on their way home. A carpenter with a box of tools on his shoulder. Two children running ahead of their grandmother, who calls to them to slow down. A young man carrying a paper-wrapped parcel very carefully in both hands. Each of them is followed by a small circle of lantern light, moving along the ground.",
"In a doorway, a mother is sitting on the step with a baby tied on her back, rocking gently from side to side and humming the same few notes of a lullaby, over and over, very quietly. The baby's eyes are already closed. A neighbour passing by stops to look, smiles, and walks on without a word, so as not to wake it.",
"Up on the second floor of one of the shops, a lamp moves behind a paper window as someone carries it from one room to another. A shadow passes across the paper, stoops, and straightens again. It is the shop's young apprentice, laying out his bedding in the little upper room where he sleeps, tired from a long day of carrying parcels and sweeping floors.",
]),

dict(title="Steam and Hot Water", scene="lanes", paras=[
"Above the roofs at the far end of the lane, a thin column of steam is rising into the moonlight. It comes from the chimney of the neighbourhood bathhouse. Most of the small homes in Edo have no bath of their own, so in the evening, after work, half the street comes here to wash and soak and talk before bed.",
"A curtain hangs over the bathhouse door, with the character for hot water dyed into it. Inside, at a raised wooden counter, an attendant sits collecting the coins. The price of a bath is a handful of small copper coins, about ten of them, less than a bowl of noodles. A child pays even less. It is one of the small, reliable pleasures of the city.",
"You leave your sandals at the door, undress in a wooden-floored room with shelves and baskets for your clothes, and step into the washing area. The floor is wooden boards, slightly sloped so the water runs away. You scoop warm water from a tub with a little wooden bucket and pour it over your shoulders, once, twice, and the cool of the evening washes away with it.",
"The bath itself is kept behind a low partition, with an opening so low that you must stoop almost double to pass through it. This keeps the heat and steam inside. Beyond it, the room is warm and dim and full of mist, so thick that you can barely see the faces of the other bathers, only shapes moving slowly in the lamplight.",
"You lower yourself into the hot water, very slowly, a little at a time, and let it rise around you up to your chin. The heat goes into your shoulders, your back, the backs of your legs. Somebody nearby lets out a long, contented sigh. Somebody else is humming a few lines of a song under their breath. Otherwise, it is quiet.",
"Afterwards, wrapped in a light cotton robe, you sit for a while on a bench in the cool air near the door, letting the warmth slowly leave your skin. An old man beside you is fanning himself with a round paper fan, slowly, back and forth. Neither of you says anything. There is no need to.",
"When you step out into the lane again, the night air feels fresh and clean, and your body feels heavy and loose, the way it does after a long hot soak. Your sandals click softly on the packed earth. The steam rises behind you from the chimney, white against the stars, thinner now as the fires under the great tub are allowed to burn low.",
"On the walls of the changing room hang wooden boards painted with notices: a shop that sells combs and hairpins, a pharmacist with a well-known remedy for coughs, a new play opening soon at one of the theatres. People read them as they dress, and talk them over. The bathhouse is where the news of the neighbourhood is passed quietly from one person to the next.",
"In many bathhouses, a narrow wooden stair leads to a room upstairs, where men can sit on the tatami after their bath, sip a cup of tea, and play a slow game of go, the black and white stones clicking softly on the board. From the window they can look down on the lane below and watch the lanterns passing, one by one, on their way home.",
]),

dict(title="The Fourth Bell", scene="pagoda", paras=[
"Let us travel now, in the easy way that happens in dreams, upstream to Asakusa, to the temple of Kannon whose statue was found in the river. The great gate is shut and the stalls along the approach are closed and shuttered. The wide gravel courtyard is empty, except for the moonlight, and a few pigeons asleep under the eaves.",
"Beside the main hall, the five-storey pagoda rises into the night sky. It has five roofs, one above the other, each a little smaller than the one below, each curving gently upward at its corners. On top stands a tall bronze spire with nine rings, pointing at the stars. This pagoda was raised in 1648, with help from the shogun himself, after fire had taken the one before.",
"Along the paths, stone lanterns stand on their thick stone legs, each with a small room at the top where a flame can be set. A monk has lit a few of them, and their little square windows glow a soft orange in the dark. Around their bases, moss has grown in the cracks between the stones, deep green even at night.",
"In its open belfry, the temple bell hangs still and heavy, a great bronze shape with a wooden beam beside it, hung on ropes, for striking. Soon it will be time. A monk in a dark robe climbs the few steps to the platform, takes the rope in both hands, and waits, listening to the bell before his, far off across the roofs, finishing its count.",
"Then he draws back the beam and lets it swing. The bell speaks, low and long, and the sound does not end so much as fade, very slowly, humming in the air and in the wooden boards beneath your feet. Three warning strokes. Then the hour. It is the fourth hour of the night, and across the city, the neighbourhood gates are being closed and barred.",
"Near the temple, a seller of singing insects is packing up for the night. His stall is a wooden frame hung with dozens of tiny bamboo cages, no bigger than your hand. Inside them are bell crickets and pine crickets, and each kind has its own song. All evening, people have stopped here to listen, and to choose one to take home.",
"Even now, as he wraps the cages in a cloth, the crickets keep singing. The bell crickets make a sound like a tiny silver bell being shaken, very fast, over and over. The pine crickets answer with a softer, two-note chirp. In autumn, people in Edo walk out to the edges of the city just to sit in the grass and listen to them, the way others go to look at blossoms in spring.",
"By the temple wall, a maple tree has turned the colour of embers. Every now and then a single leaf lets go and spins slowly down through the lantern light, and settles on the gravel without a sound. Beside it, a clump of tall pampas grass has opened its silver plumes, and they lean together in the moonlight, nodding, as if they were half asleep.",
"The moon is high now, and its light falls across the courtyard so brightly that the pagoda lays a long shadow over the gravel, five roofs stacked in dark silhouette. A few weeks ago, on the night of the harvest moon, families all over the city set out little white rice dumplings and sprays of pampas grass on their verandas as an offering to the moon. In some houses, the grass is still standing in its vase tonight.",
"Down by the river, below the temple, the water is black and very still, and the moon lies on it in a long, broken line. A heron stands in the shallows on one leg, perfectly motionless, the way it has been standing for an hour or more. It is not fishing now. It is simply resting, the way you are resting, with nowhere at all to be.",
]),

dict(title="Behind the Paper Screen", scene="tatami", paras=[
"Now we go home, back through the quiet streets, through the wicket of the ward gate, past the nodding gatekeeper, and down a narrow alley between long wooden row houses. Each house is a single room with a little earth-floored kitchen by the door. You slide the door open on its wooden groove, step up out of your sandals, and you are inside.",
"The floor is covered with tatami mats, thick and firm and woven from rush, with dark cloth edges. They smell faintly of dry summer grass. Along one side of the room, the windows are covered with paper screens, and the moonlight shines through them, so that the whole wall glows a soft pale blue, with the shadow of a pine branch from the alley laid across it.",
"In the corner stands a paper floor lamp, a tall wooden frame with paper stretched around it and a small dish of oil inside, with a wick of pith resting in it. Better-off families burn rapeseed oil, which gives a clear, steady light. Poorer ones burn cheaper sardine oil, which smells a little of the sea. Tonight the lamp is turned low, and its light is warm and gentle.",
"In the middle of the room sits a round brazier of glazed clay, half full of fine ash, with a few pieces of charcoal glowing red in the centre. An iron kettle rests on a trivet above them, and a thin curl of steam rises from its spout. The water inside is just beginning to murmur, making a low, soft sound like wind in distant pines.",
"You pour a little of the hot water into a pot of roasted tea, and then into a small cup with no handle. Hold it in both hands for a moment. Feel the warmth go into your palms and up through your wrists. The tea is brown and smoky and very mild. You drink it slowly, and the warmth spreads out inside you.",
"On a low shelf by the window, the little bamboo cage from the temple sits beside a vase of pampas grass. The bell cricket inside has settled in, and every so often it begins to sing again, a tiny shimmering sound that fills the whole room. Then it stops, as though listening, and then it begins again.",
"Now it is time to lay out the bedding. From a cupboard behind a sliding door, you lift down the thick padded mattress and unfold it on the tatami. Over it goes a heavy quilt, soft and quilted with cotton wadding. The pillow is small and firm, filled with buckwheat husks that shift and settle under your head with a quiet, sandy whisper.",
"You turn the lamp down until only a small bead of flame remains, glowing through the paper like a little moon of its own. Lie back. Feel the firm tatami beneath the mattress, and the weight of the quilt settling over you. Through the thin walls, you can hear the neighbours moving about, then a murmur of voices, then nothing at all.",
"On the wall hangs a single scroll, with a heron standing among reeds, painted in a few strokes of grey ink. Below it, on a narrow shelf, there is a small household altar with a bowl of rice, a cup of water and a sprig of evergreen, set out for the family's ancestors. A thin stick of incense was lit there earlier, and its faint sweet smell still hangs in the air.",
"The house is small, but it holds everything that is needed. A chest with the family's robes folded inside. A few pots and bowls in the kitchen. A wooden tub for rinsing rice. By day, with the bedding put away, this same room is a workshop, a dining room, and a place to sit with friends. At night it becomes a bedroom, simply by unfolding the quilts.",
]),

dict(title="The Night Watch", scene="lanes", paras=[
"Out in the lane, under the moon, an old man is walking slowly from one end of the neighbourhood to the other. He wears a short padded jacket and carries a lantern on a pole. In his other hand he holds two blocks of hard wood, tied together with a cord. He is the night watchman, and he has walked this route every night for many years.",
"Every so often he stops, raises the blocks, and claps them together twice. Clack, clack. The sound is sharp and clean, and it carries a long way between the wooden houses. Then he calls out, in a long, sing-song voice, a gentle reminder to the sleeping street to take care with their fires and lamps. Then two more claps, and he walks on.",
"In a city built of wood and paper, this is a comfort to hear. It means that someone is awake, and watching, and that all is well. Mothers half wake, hear the clappers, and close their eyes again. Children who are still awake listen to the sound passing under their windows, fading slowly down the lane, and feel safe enough to sleep.",
"The lantern maker's shop is dark now. The lanterns under his eaves have been taken in and folded flat, and only one is left burning, low, above the door. The bathhouse chimney gives one last thin breath of steam and then stops. The gate at the end of the lane is shut, and inside his little hut the gatekeeper sits by his brazier, his chin slowly sinking toward his chest.",
"On a rooftop, a cat walks along the ridge of the tiles, perfectly balanced, and then sits down at the very end of the roof to wash one paw. Below it, in the alley, a small dog curls up on a straw mat outside a doorway, sighs, and closes its eyes. Somewhere a door slides shut, softly, on its wooden groove.",
"Far across the city, the soba seller under the willow is packing away his bowls into the wooden boxes of his stall. He lifts the whole thing onto his shoulder by its pole and sets off for home, and as he walks, the little wind chime swings and rings, faint and clear, fading away down the riverbank until there is only the river.",
"And now the clappers again, very far away, at the other end of the neighbourhood. Clack, clack. And the long, soft call. And then, from much farther still, from another watchman in another lane, an answering clack, so faint it might be a dream. And over all of it, more than a million people, asleep under the same moon.",
"The watchman's route takes him past the shuttered shops, along the canal, and back through the alleys. He knows every doorway. He knows which house has a new baby, and he claps a little more softly as he passes it. He knows which old woman likes to hear him go by before she sleeps, and he calls a little more slowly beneath her window.",
"Overhead, the stars turn slowly across the sky above the roofs. The moon has passed over the fire watchtower and begun its long slide down toward the west, toward the castle and the far mountains. The hours of the night are long in autumn, and there are many of them still to come before the first grey light of morning.",
]),

dict(title="Goodnight, Edo", scene="tatami", paras=[
"You can let go now. The bells will keep the hours for you. The watchman will walk the lanes for you. The gates are closed, the curtains are in, and the lamps have been turned down all across the city. There is nothing left to do tonight but rest, as deeply and as long as you like.",
"Let your breathing slow until it is as easy as the swaying of the willow over the river. Let your body grow as warm and heavy as it did in the hot bath. And let your thoughts drift away like the pleasure boat on the Sumida, growing smaller and softer, until they are only a faint golden glow on dark water.",
"Outside the paper screen, the pine shadow moves very slightly on the moonlit wall. The bell cricket sings its small silver song, and then rests. Far away, beyond the castle and the hills, the snow on Mount Fuji shines faintly under the moon, keeping watch over the sleeping city, as it has for a very long time.",
"Thank you for spending this autumn night in Edo with The Quiet Archive. The lamp is low, the kettle is quiet, and the whole city is dreaming. Sleep well. Goodnight.",
]),
]
